use /lib
from /scope/open-projects import OpenProjectsScope
from /scope/prompt-mode/chat-input import ChatInputScope
from /scope/prompt-mode/modes import PromptModesScope
from /ui/components/history-list import HistoryListScope

export shape MessageListScrollingShape:
    description:
        The latest task's table scrolls with its lock to the bottom off to
        start with, until switched on. Each open project keeps how far its
        latest task's table was scrolled and whether it was locked, and the
        rest of its message list as it was left, as the @{OpenProjectsScope}
        says, so switching back to it shows the list just as it was.

        However long the list of previous tasks grows, drawing it costs no more
        than drawing the tasks in view, so scrolling, animations, and new output
        stay smooth.

    scrollingPast:
        Scrolling carries on past either end of the latest task and the
        previous tasks, from one to the other, through an elastic stretch at
        that end. A scroll that reaches an end doesn't stop dead there: the
        same scroll keeps going, with no pause needed, into the stretch, which
        resists it, and it crosses over only once it has pushed far enough
        into the stretch to get past a barrier at its far side. The only
        scroll that stops at an end is a fling's momentum still running when
        it gets there, and a scroll carried on from a slide (below).

        The barrier is a distance to scroll past. The distance a scroll goes
        past the end builds up, and it crosses over the moment that reaches
        200 pixels, about two notches of a mouse wheel or a short swipe on a
        trackpad, however quickly or slowly it gets there. Scrolling back the
        other way takes the build-up down again, and scrolls the view once it
        is gone. The build-up drains away on its own once the scrolling
        pauses, back to nothing within half a second of the last scroll, so a
        nudge into the stretch that goes no further never crosses over later.
        Momentum, and any scroll during a slide, add nothing to it.

        The stretch is felt as well as seen. While anything has built up, the
        view at that end is pulled past its end, following the scroll but by
        less than it scrolled, and by less the further it is pulled, as a
        rubber band gives: at the start it moves nearly as far as the scroll,
        and by the barrier it has been pulled about 64 pixels, never more.
        Scrolling up at the latest task's top, its header and output move down
        together, leaving an empty band above them, in the body's colour;
        scrolling down at the previous tasks' bottom, they move up, leaving
        the band below them. The previous tasks row and the queue stay where
        they are, and nothing is drawn outside the space between them. The
        pull follows the build-up as it grows and drains, smoothly rather than
        in steps, so as the build-up drains the view springs back to its end,
        settling without bouncing. The view's own scroll position doesn't
        change while it is pulled; it is still at its end once it springs
        back.

        While anything has built up against an end, a glow shows where the
        crossing will happen: a soft radial glow, centred in the space between
        the previous tasks row and the queue, fading from its middle to
        nothing at its edge, in the colour of the mode selected in the
        @{ChatInputScope}, as the @{PromptModesScope} colours it. It starts
        small and faint and grows larger and slightly brighter as the build-up
        nears the barrier, at its largest a little under the height of that
        space and still only a subtle wash of colour, never hiding what is
        beneath it. It follows the build-up as it grows and drains, smoothly
        rather than in steps. It never takes a click or a scroll. As the view
        crosses over, the glow fades out quickly while the slide begins; with
        nothing built up, there is no glow at all.

        With the latest task's output scrolled to its top, a scroll up
        past the barrier expands the previous tasks, as clicking their row does, opening
        scrolled to the bottom, on the tasks sent most recently, as the
        @{HistoryListScope} says. Before any task has been sent, or with no previous
        tasks, scrolling up does nothing more: the view stops at its top
        without stretching, and no glow shows.

        With the previous tasks expanded and scrolled to their bottom, a
        scroll down past the barrier closes them, as clicking "Back to the latest task" does,
        bringing back the latest task and its output as they were left.

        However the previous tasks are closed, by a scroll or by clicking
        "Back to the latest task", the latest task's output comes back
        scrolled just where it was when they were opened, with its lock to the
        bottom as it was. Locked, it comes back at its bottom and still
        locked, showing whatever output arrived while the previous tasks were
        open, and goes on following new output. Unlocked, it comes back at
        the same place in its output, however much arrived meanwhile; if that
        place no longer exists, as when the latest task has changed since, it
        comes back at its top, unlocked.

        A fling's momentum still running when an end is reached stops there,
        never pulling into the stretch or crossing over. Output or a
        list too short to scroll is always at both its ends, so any scroll
        over it that leads somewhere pulls into the stretch. Only the wheel or trackpad
        crosses over. Scrolling sideways, dragging a scrollbar to an end, and
        scrolling anything inside that scrolls on its own, such as an open
        task's table in the list, never do.

        Crossing over by scrolling is animated, starting from wherever the
        stretch has pulled the view. Scrolling up, the previous
        tasks slide down into view from above, already scrolled to their
        bottom, pushing the latest task's header and output out below them, the
        two moving together as one column. Scrolling down, the latest task's
        header and output slide up into view from below, already scrolled where
        they will settle,
        pushing the previous tasks out above them. The slide is quick and
        settles without bouncing, as the right sidebar's does. Only the space
        between the previous tasks row and the queue moves, and nothing is
        drawn outside it; the row and the queue stay where they are, the row reading what it will once the slide settles, from the
        moment it starts. While it slides, the wheel scrolls neither view, and
        no scroll then crosses back. A scroll carried on from the slide stops
        at the end it lands on rather than pulling into the stretch, so one
        long scroll never crosses twice; a new scroll, begun once the wheel or
        trackpad has been still for 80 milliseconds, counted from the last
        scroll whether that came during the slide or after, or once fingers
        are lifted from the trackpad and put back, pulls into the stretch as
        usual. Scrolling during the slide doesn't make that wait any longer.
        Expanding or closing the previous tasks by clicking their row is not
        animated.
